==English==

===Etymology 1===
The letter name is from {{inh|en|enm|a|ā}}, from {{der|en|fro|-}}, ultimately from {{der|en|la|ā}}. Use of the Latin letter in (Old) English displaced the [[futhorc]] letter {{m|ang|ᚪ}} beginning in the 7th century, and partially also {{m|ang|ᚫ}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{q|letter name}}
** {{IPA|en|/eɪ/|a=UK,US}}
*** {{audio|en|en-us-a.ogg|a=US}}
** {{IPA|en|/æɪ/|a=AusE}}
** {{IPA|en|[eː]|a=CA}}
** {{rhymes|en|eɪ|s=1}}
*: The current pronunciation resulted from the [[w:Great Vowel Shift|Great Vowel Shift]]. Before the early part of the 17th century, the pronunciation was similar to that in other languages.
* {{q|phoneme}} {{IPA|en|/æ/|/ɑː/|/eɪ/|/ə/}}, etc.

====Letter====
{{letter|en}}

# {{Latn-def|en|letter|1|a}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:John Wesley Young|author2=Frank Millett Morgan|title=Elementary Mathematical Analysis|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=w:The Macmillan Company|year=1917|page=487|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/elementarymathem00younrich/page/487/mode/1up|passage=This expression is zero, for we have replaced the column of '''''a''{{'}}s''' by the column of ''b''{{'}}s and hence the determinant has two columns identical.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Ervin A. Dennis|author2=John D. Jenkins|chapter=A Font of Type|title=Comprehensive Graphic Arts|location=Indianapolis, I.N.|publisher=w:Howard W. Sams & Co., Inc.|year=1974|page=26|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/comprehensivegra00denn/page/26/mode/1up|column=2|isbn=0672208865|passage=Note that with 18-point type, fifteen capital ''A{{'}}s'', twenty-five lowercase '''''a{{'}}s''''', and twelve ''1{{'}}s'' are obtained with one font. With this information, it is possible to refer to Table 10-1 which gives the number of characters for each letter, punctuation mark, or figure.}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|author=w:Rivka Galchen|title=What's Become of the So-Called Literary Bad Boy?|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20140219053900id_/http://www.nytimes.com/2014/02/23/books/review/whats-become-of-the-so-called-literary-bad-boy.html|date=23 February 2014|passage=In the seventh grade I admired a charismatic, witty girl who had a particular way of writing her lowercase '''a's'''. After some practice, I took to writing my lowercase '''a's''' in the same fashion.}}

=====Usage notes=====
* In English, the letter ''a'' usually denotes the [[w:near-open front unrounded vowel|near-open front unrounded vowel]] ({{IPA|en|/æ/}}), as in ''[[pad]]'', the [[w:open back unrounded vowel|open back unrounded vowel]] ({{IPA|en|/ɑː/}}) as in ''[[father]]'', the [[w:mid-central vowel|mid-central vowel]] ({{IPA|en|/ə/}}) when unstressed as in ''[[about]]'', or, when followed by another vowel, the diphthong {{IPA|en|/eɪ/}}, as in ''[[ace]]''.
* ''A'' is the third most common letter in English.

